---
import { Icon } from 'astro-icon/components';
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import MCQuiz from '../../components/MCQuiz.astro';
---

<Layout title="Which line actually runs the SQL?" description="Practice card. Five lines of ActiveRecord chaining, only one talks to the database. Drills Relation laziness.">
  <article class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-6 py-12 space-y-6">
    <div>
      <a href="/" class="text-ruby hover:text-ruby-dark transition-colors inline-flex items-center gap-2 mb-6">
        <span>←</span> Back to Course
      </a>
      <p class="text-sm uppercase tracking-wider text-ruby font-semibold mb-2">Practice · Reading the Source · Card 2</p>
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-ruby-dark mb-4">Which line actually runs the SQL?</h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-600">Five lines of <code class="bg-gray-100 px-2 py-1 rounded text-base">User.where(...)</code> chaining. Only one of them hits the database. Pick it.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2 gap-6 items-start">
      <div class="bg-white rounded-lg shadow-sm border border-gray-200 p-8 space-y-6">
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3 flex items-center gap-2">
            <Icon name="lucide:search" class="w-5 h-5 text-gray-500 inline-block" aria-hidden="true" /> The code
          </h3>
          <p class="text-gray-700 italic mb-3">Reading top to bottom, which line is the moment the database wakes up?</p>
          <div class="border-2 border-gray-200 rounded-lg p-4 bg-gray-50/40">
            <CodeBlock code={`def call
  users = User.where(active: true)            # line 1
  users = users.where(role: "admin")           # line 2
  users = users.order(:created_at).limit(10)   # line 3
  Rails.logger.info "Found admins"             # line 4
  users.each { |u| send_email(u) }             # line 5
end`} language="ruby" />
          </div>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">The question</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Which of the five lines is the first to send SQL to the database?</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-white rounded-lg shadow-sm border border-gray-200 p-8 space-y-6 lg:sticky lg:top-6">
        <MCQuiz
          options={[
            {
              label: 'A',
              text: 'Line 1 — <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">User.where(active: true)</code>',
              explanation: '<code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">where</code> returns an <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">ActiveRecord::Relation</code>. The Relation is a description of a query, not a query result. No SQL has run.',
            },
            {
              label: 'B',
              text: 'Line 2 — chained <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">.where(role: "admin")</code>',
              explanation: 'Chaining another <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">where</code> just produces a new Relation with both conditions merged into its spec. Still no SQL.',
            },
            {
              label: 'C',
              text: 'Line 3 — chained <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">.order(:created_at).limit(10)</code>',
              explanation: 'Same story. <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">order</code> and <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">limit</code> add to the Relation\'s spec and return a new Relation. No database call yet.',
            },
            {
              label: 'D',
              text: 'Line 5 — <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">users.each { ... }</code>',
              explanation: '<code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">.each</code> is the first method here that needs the actual rows. That\'s when Rails finally runs the SQL, loads the results into memory, and yields each User to the block.',
              correct: true,
            },
          ]}
          principle={`<p>A Relation is lazy. It collects spec (where, order, limit, includes, joins) without touching the database. It only runs SQL when something asks for the actual data: <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">.each</code>, <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">.to_a</code>, <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">.first</code>, <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">.count</code>, <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">.pluck</code>, <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">.exists?</code>, <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">.find_each</code>, or interpolation into a view (which calls <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">.to_a</code>).</p><p>Methods that return a new Relation (where, order, limit, joins, includes, select, group, having) do not run SQL. Knowing the split lets you compose queries safely over many lines without performance worry, and lets you call <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">.to_sql</code> to inspect what the SQL will look like before triggering it.</p>`}
          theoryHref="/lessons/source-activerecord-query"
          theoryLabel="Reading the Source · Card 3 — How User.where(...) becomes SQL"
        />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="flex justify-between items-center pt-4">
      <a href="/lessons/practice-source-belongs-to-optional" class="text-ruby hover:text-ruby-dark transition-colors">← Card 1</a>
      <a href="/lessons/practice-source-require-vs-bracket" class="text-ruby hover:text-ruby-dark transition-colors">Card 3 →</a>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
